[32m+[m[32m## Invocation[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:12: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:13: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32mTwo choices, trading different costs:[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:14: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:15: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m- A **model-invoked** skill keeps a **description**, so the agent can fire it autonomously _and_ other skills can reach it (you can still type its name too). It contributes to **context load** — the description sits in the window every turn. Mechanics: omit `disable-model-invocation`, and write a model-facing description with rich trigger phrasing ("Use when the user wants…, mentions…").[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:16: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m- A **user-invoked** skill strips the description from the agent's reach: only you, typing its name, can invoke it — and no other skill can. Zero context load, but it spends **cognitive load**: _you_ are the index that must remember it exists. Mechanics: set `disable-model-invocation: true`; the `description` becomes human-facing — a one-line summary, trigger lists stripped.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:17: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:18: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32mPick model-invocation only when the agent must reach the skill on its own, or another skill must. If it only ever fires by hand, make it user-invoked and pay no context load.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:19: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:20: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32mWhen user-invoked skills multiply past what you can remember, that piled-up cognitive load is cured by a **router skill**: one user-invoked skill that names the others and when to reach for each.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:21: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:22: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m## Writing the description[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:23: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:24: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32mA model-invoked **description** does two jobs — state what the skill is, and list the **branches** that should trigger it. Every word increases **context load**, so a description earns even harder pruning than the body:[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:25: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:26: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m- **Front-load the skill's leading word** — the description is where it does its invocation work.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:27: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m- **One trigger per branch.** Synonyms that rename a single branch are **duplication** — "build features using TDD … asks for test-first development" is one branch written twice. Collapse them; keep only genuinely distinct branches.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:28: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m- **Cut identity that's already in the body.** Keep the description to triggers, plus any "when another skill needs…" reach clause.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:29: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:30: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m## Information hierarchy[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:31: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:32: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32mA skill is built from two content types — **steps** and **reference** — that mix freely: a skill can be all steps, all reference, or both. The core decision is which to use and where each sits on the **information hierarchy**, a ladder ranked by how immediately the agent needs the material:[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:33: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:34: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m1. **In-skill step** — an ordered action in `SKILL.md`, the primary tier: what the agent does, in order. Each step ends on a **completion criterion**, the condition that tells the agent the work is done. Make it _checkable_ (can the agent tell done from not-done?) and, where it matters, _exhaustive_ ("every modified model accounted for", not "produce a change list") — a vague criterion invites **premature completion**.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:35: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m2. **In-skill reference** — a definition, rule, or fact in `SKILL.md`, consulted on demand. Often a legitimately flat peer-set (every rule of a review on one rung) — a fine arrangement, not a smell. _This skill is all reference._[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:36: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m3. **External reference** — reference pushed out of `SKILL.md` into a separate file, reached by a **context pointer**, loaded only when the pointer fires. (Spans _disclosed_ reference — a sibling file like `GLOSSARY.md`, still part of the skill — through fully **external reference** that lives outside the skill system and any skill can point at.)[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:37: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:38: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32mA demanding completion criterion drives thorough **legwork** — the digging the agent does within the work — whether the skill has steps or not, since "every rule applied" binds flat reference just as "every step done" binds a sequence.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:39: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:40: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32mPush too little down and the top bloats; push too much and you hide material the agent actually needs. That tension is the whole decision.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:41: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:42: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m**Progressive disclosure** is the move down the ladder — out of `SKILL.md` into a linked file — so the top stays legible. Mechanics: a linked `.md` file in the skill folder, named for what it holds (this skill discloses its full definitions to `GLOSSARY.md`). Some skills are used in more than one way, and each distinct way is a **branch** — different runs taking different paths through the skill. Branching is the cleanest disclosure test: inline what every branch needs, and push behind a pointer what only some branches reach. A **context pointer**'s _wording_, not its target, decides when and how reliably the agent reaches the material.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:43: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:44: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32mWhere the ladder decides _how far down_ a piece sits, **co-location** decides _what sits beside it_ once there: keep a concept's definition, rules, and caveats under one heading rather than scattered, so reading one part brings its neighbours with it.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:45: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:46: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m## When to split[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:47: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:48: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m**Granularity** is how finely you divide skills, and each cut spends one of the two loads, so split only when the cut earns it. Two cuts:[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:49: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:50: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m- **By invocation** — split off a **model-invoked** skill when you have a distinct **leading word** that should trigger it on its own, or another skill must reach it. You pay **context load** for the new always-loaded **description**, so that independent reach has to be worth it.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:51: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m- **By sequence** — split a run of **steps** when the steps still ahead (a step's **post-completion steps**) tempt the agent to rush the one in front of it (**premature completion**). Keeping them out of view encourages the agent to do more **legwork** on the current task.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:52: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:53: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m## Pruning[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:54: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:55: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32mKeep each meaning in a **single source of truth**: one authoritative place, so changing the behaviour is a one-place edit.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:56: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:57: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32mCheck every line for **relevance**: does it still bear on what the skill does?[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:58: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:59: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32mThen hunt **no-ops** sentence by sentence, not just line by line: run the no-op test on each sentence in isolation, and when one fails, delete the whole sentence rather than trim words from it. Be aggressive — most prose that fails should go, not be rewritten.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:60: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:61: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m## Leading words[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:62: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:63: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32mA **leading word** is a compact concept already living in the model's pretraining that the agent thinks with while running the skill (e.g. _lesson_, _fog of war_, _tracer bullets_). Repeated throughout the text (though not necessarily - a strong leading word might only be needed once), it accumulates a distributed definition and anchors a whole region of behaviour in the fewest tokens, by recruiting priors the model already holds.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:64: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:65: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32mIt serves predictability twice. In the body it anchors _execution_: the agent reaches for the same behaviour every time the word appears. In the description it anchors _invocation_: when the same word lives in your prompts, docs, and code, the agent links that shared language to the skill and fires it more reliably.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:66: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:67: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32mHunt for opportunities to refactor skills to use leading words. A triad spelled out at three sites (**duplication**), a description spending a sentence to gesture at one idea — each is a passage begging to **collapse** into a single token. Examples include:[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:68: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:69: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m- "fast, deterministic, low-overhead" -> _tight_ — one quality restated across a phase — into a single pretrained word (a _tight_ loop).[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:70: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m- "a loop you believe in" -> _red_ — converts a fuzzy gate into a binary observable state (the loop goes _red_ on the bug, or it doesn't).[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:71: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:72: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32mYou win twice over: fewer tokens, _and_ a sharper hook for the agent to hang its thinking on. Assume every skill is carrying restatements that leading words retire — go find them.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:73: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:74: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m## Failure modes[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:75: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:76: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32mUse these to diagnose issues the user may be having with the skill.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:77: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:78: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m- **Premature completion** — ending a step before it's genuinely done, attention slipping to _being done_. Defence, in order: sharpen the completion criterion first (cheap, local); only if it is irreducibly fuzzy _and_ you observe the rush, hide the post-completion steps by splitting (the sequence cut).[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:79: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m- **Duplication** — the same meaning in more than one place. Costs maintenance and tokens, and inflates a meaning's prominence on the ladder past its real rank.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:80: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m- **Sediment** — stale layers that settle because adding feels safe and removing feels risky. The default fate of any skill without a pruning discipline.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:81: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m- **Sprawl** — a skill simply too long, even when every line is live and unique. Hurts readability and maintainability and wastes tokens. The cure is the ladder: disclose **reference** behind pointers, and split by **branch** or sequence so each path carries only what it needs.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:82: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m- **No-op** — a line the model already obeys by default, so you pay load to say nothing. The test: does it change behaviour versus the default? A weak leading word (_be thorough_ when the agent is already thorough-ish) is a no-op; the fix is a stronger word (_relentless_), not a different technique.[m[41m[m
skills/productivity/writing-great-skills/SKILL.md:83: trailing whitespace.
[32m+[m[32m- **Negation** — steering by prohibition backfires: _don't think of an elephant_ names the elephant and makes it more available, not less. Prompt the **positive** — state the target behaviour so the banned one is never spoken; keep a prohibition only as a hard guardrail you can't phrase positively, and even then pair it with what to do instead.[m[41m[m
